2186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1031 "Constructing Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1033 3721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7931e951-f0f8-5132-97e2-67fa64802a65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1031 "Factoring Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1033 87 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7f29909c-8f45-5a17-820a-13a0a327cd5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $9a$, $18a,$ and $27a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7bc66df5-26b7-5eb0-b756-f8b086607209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $9$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $18$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 3,\\quad 6,\\quad 9,\\quad 18.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $27$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9,\\quad 27.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $9,$ $18,$ and $27$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $9.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. The expressions $9a,$ $18a,$ and $27a$ all contain $a.$\n\nThus, the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $9a,$ $18a,$ and $27a$ is $9a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $4a$ and $15b$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa1c4cbd-2086-5634-809f-43e4dd6becf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n15b &= 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we try to pair off the individual factors, we can't find any common factors. The only common factor is one. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(4a, 15b) = 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1069 "e-816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $\\textrm{GCF}(10k,20)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ce77198-a3b2-5e9b-98d5-db8922addc1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $10$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 5,\\quad 10.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $20$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 4,\\quad 5,\\quad 10,\\quad 20.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $10$ and $20$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 5,\\quad 10.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $10.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Since $20$ has no variable and $10k$ contains $k,$ there are no variable factors in common.\n\nThus, $\\textrm{GCF}(10k,20)=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1069 "e-817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the greatest common factor of the terms $33p$ and $22p.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4d78520-1050-5ca1-a2da-1b813269dd9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $33$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 11,\\quad 33.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $22$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 11,\\quad 22.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $33$ and $22$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 11.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $11.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Both $33p$ and $22p$ contain $p.$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $33p$ and $22p$ is $11p.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186459236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186459247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of a Number and a Variable Expression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "92176bb0-84dc-5fa4-8154-e974c05fff06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054688 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055263 #edb/ref 1759218604541